WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.200
So originally, the
plan was to talk more

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:09.660
about reinforcement learning.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:12.340
But I thought since I only
had one lecture to give,

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:14.640
I think this class
would be a little bit

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:17.780
incomplete without saying
something about multimodality,

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:21.880
which is so pervasive if you
look at all the major models.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:27.040
So this will be an overview
of multimodal models.

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:29.960
Of course, this could be
a whole class in itself,

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:32.759
and I'll try to do what
I can in one lecture.

00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:35.500
OK, so let's dive into
multimodal models.

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:38.280
So far in this class,
we have exclusively

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:40.240
focused on language models.

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:43.300
And language models are
already pretty general.

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:45.760
They give you the ability
to go from any piece of text

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:47.180
to any text.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:53.340
So this could be any language,
code, poetry, anything you like,

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:57.680
even, let's say,
DNA, for example.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:00.380
But the world is multi-modal.

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:05.260
You have text, you have
images, audio, video.

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:09.820
And if you think about a North
Star of where we want to be

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:12.580
is what people call
omni model, which

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:17.220
is ability to take any
combination of these modalities

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:20.860
and output any combination
of any of these modalities.

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:24.500
So, for example, I can give
you an image and a video

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:29.920
and I can ask a question
about the image and the video.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:31.080
I can generate images.

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:34.400
I can convert the
audio into an image.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:37.140
You can imagine all sorts
of different applications

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:39.000
by having an omni model.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.620
So in this class, I'm
not going to build up

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:42.920
to a whole omni model.

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:47.900
But I'll give you some of the
pieces that at least to allow

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:52.900
you to ingest a little bit
of images, which we'll mostly

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:55.900
focus on today.

00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:57.990
So how do you
build such a thing?

00:01:57.990 --> 00:02:02.210
Well, this is a weird
way to motivate this,

00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:06.070
but the reality is that
transformers work really well.

00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:10.630
And despite the best efforts
of people to try other things,

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:14.410
those are across all of these
modalities still at scale

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:15.690
the best thing we have.

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:17.910
So we have to figure
out how to use them.

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:21.190
And now, transformers
were designed for text.

00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:24.910
So they have this property
that they speak tokens.

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:27.990
So they take as input
a bunch of tokens

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:30.310
and then output some tokens.

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:34.070
And here, I'm
expanding the notion

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:36.950
of tokenization to be not just
a discrete token that we've

00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:40.730
seen in text but also
potentially continuous tokens.

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:44.830
You can think about these
as embedding of tokens.

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:46.750
And the key thing
is that a token

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:51.350
should represent some
semantic unit of information.

00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:55.390
For example, in natural
language, tokens are subwords.

00:02:55.390 --> 00:02:58.050
And these are
somewhat meaningful.

00:02:58.050 --> 00:03:02.090
Whereas, for example, a
pixel is certainly not

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:04.890
meaningful by itself.

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:07.430
So somehow we must
convert everything,

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:13.530
including audio and images, into
either discrete or continuous

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:15.370
tokens.

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:18.450
Now, notice that we've already
had to do this even with text.

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:21.570
In the first lecture, we
talked about tokenization.

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:24.830
And there, it was not too bad.

00:03:24.830 --> 00:03:26.750
I mean, we had a BP tokenizer.

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:30.010
You guys implemented it.

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:31.210
It's fine.

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:37.690
One could wish for a better
tokenizer or something that

00:03:37.690 --> 00:03:39.590
doesn't have tokenizers at all.

00:03:39.590 --> 00:03:43.810
But it's not the worst
thing in the world.

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:46.530
Now, for non-text
modalities, we have

00:03:46.530 --> 00:03:49.410
to scratch our heads a
lot more and figure out

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:51.810
what is the equivalent
of the BP tokenizer that

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:54.020
will take an image
and produce things

00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:57.340
that a transformer can digest?

00:03:57.340 --> 00:04:01.280
OK, so you have to answer
two questions here.

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:04.460
One is, for non-text data,
for example, images or videos

00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:09.700
or audio, how do you input
these into the transformer?

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:12.740
And then how do you
generate them out?

00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:17.579
And this lecture will mostly
focus on question number one.

00:04:17.579 --> 00:04:25.860
OK, so let's start back and
rewind the clock back to 21.

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:28.980
So the CLIP model, which some
of you might be familiar with,

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:34.260
is a lot of the
foundation of modern VLMs.

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:39.780
And so we can go through
this a bit in more detail.

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:43.500
So CLIP stands for Contrastive
Language Image Pre-Training.

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:46.460
And a bit of historical
context is around

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:52.040
that time GPT-3 had
already happened.

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:52.840
GPT-2.

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:55.480
All the language
models had already

00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:59.200
gone into this
foundation model era.

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:03.220
Neovision was still--
there were some efforts,

00:05:03.220 --> 00:05:08.980
but traditionally had been
based on large annotated data

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:12.760
sets, such as ImageNet,
and training models,

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:15.920
such as the ResNet,
on these data sets

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:19.160
and doing a bunch of other
things, like data augmentation,

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:21.720
and getting really
good performance.

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:25.960
And so the researchers
at OpenAI were wondering,

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:29.520
is it possible to leverage
the large amount of image

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:32.480
and textual captions out there?

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:35.840
So inspired by this idea in
language where you could just

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:39.460
scrape the internet, you have
all this text, it's very noisy.

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:42.180
But somehow if you train
a large enough model,

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:45.680
you can make sense out of this
and generate something useful.

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:49.720
So what was the equivalent
of that for images?

00:05:49.720 --> 00:05:53.420
So as a result, CLIP was born.

00:05:53.420 --> 00:05:58.900
And the idea of CLIP is fairly
simple and straightforward.

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:02.380
So the idea here is
that suppose I give you

00:06:02.380 --> 00:06:05.980
a bunch of image-text pairs.

00:06:05.980 --> 00:06:09.220
So let's say I give
you 32,000 of these.

00:06:09.220 --> 00:06:13.140
OK, so what I'm going
to do is for each image

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:16.900
I'm going to encode it using
an image encoder, which I'll

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:20.380
explain later, into a vector.

00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:26.380
So I'm going to have 32,000
of these image encodings.

00:06:26.380 --> 00:06:27.940
I'm going to also
do the same thing

00:06:27.940 --> 00:06:30.400
with the corresponding text.

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:33.960
So that will give
me T1 through TN.

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:36.240
So these are the
embeddings of the text.

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:39.100
These are embedding
of the images.

00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:41.220
Now, the objective
I want to set up

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:48.550
is that I want the
image-- let's take I1.

00:06:48.550 --> 00:06:52.410
So I1 is aligned with T1.

00:06:52.410 --> 00:06:57.670
So I want this alignment, the
dot product between these two

00:06:57.670 --> 00:07:01.750
embeddings to be much larger
than the dot product between I1

00:07:01.750 --> 00:07:04.590
and all the other texts, OK.

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:08.830
And conversely, I also
want for this text

00:07:08.830 --> 00:07:11.110
to say that this dot
product is larger

00:07:11.110 --> 00:07:14.590
than the dot product between
T1 and all the other images.

00:07:14.590 --> 00:07:18.930
So that, in a nutshell,
is the CLIP objective.

00:07:18.930 --> 00:07:22.270
And you can imagine
doing-- there's basically

00:07:22.270 --> 00:07:30.350
2 times N different softmax
classification problems.

00:07:30.350 --> 00:07:33.990
So here's the code.

00:07:33.990 --> 00:07:40.470
So you get a n by d matrix
for the image encodings

00:07:40.470 --> 00:07:43.870
and n by d times for
the textual embeddings.

00:07:43.870 --> 00:07:48.570
You normalize and you
take this dot stop product

00:07:48.570 --> 00:07:50.370
with some temperature.

00:07:50.370 --> 00:07:56.970
And then you compute
the cross entropy, OK.

00:07:56.970 --> 00:08:00.210
So it's basically a multi-class
classification problem,

00:08:00.210 --> 00:08:05.530
where the examples are
structured in this m

00:08:05.530 --> 00:08:09.050
by n matrix form.

00:08:09.050 --> 00:08:11.390
OK, any questions about that?

00:08:15.210 --> 00:08:24.050
All right, so some more details
about how CLIP was built.

00:08:24.050 --> 00:08:26.610
So where does the
data come from?

00:08:26.610 --> 00:08:32.890
There are not too many details
in the paper about the data.

00:08:32.890 --> 00:08:39.490
Roughly, you take a bunch
of queries from somewhere

00:08:39.490 --> 00:08:43.250
and then you go search
online for these

00:08:43.250 --> 00:08:47.630
and you mine a bunch of
image and text pairs.

00:08:47.630 --> 00:08:53.590
And this resulted in 400
million image-text pairs.

00:08:53.590 --> 00:08:55.990
The data set wasn't
released, but there

00:08:55.990 --> 00:09:00.270
was an effort by
this paper called

00:09:00.270 --> 00:09:09.110
OpenCLIP, which replicated and
further extended the CLIP ideas.

00:09:09.110 --> 00:09:12.090
So there was a data
set called LAION-5B,

00:09:12.090 --> 00:09:14.430
which was released
5 billion images

00:09:14.430 --> 00:09:16.350
with textual descriptions.

00:09:16.350 --> 00:09:19.470
And there was a bunch of
processing that happened.

00:09:19.470 --> 00:09:22.030
Interestingly, they actually
used CLIP for the data filtering

00:09:22.030 --> 00:09:24.310
and then train OpenCLIP.

00:09:24.310 --> 00:09:26.530
So there's some
bootstrapping happening.

00:09:26.530 --> 00:09:28.390
But at least this
is a model where

00:09:28.390 --> 00:09:31.350
you can point to the data
set that it was trained

00:09:31.350 --> 00:09:34.290
on and the code as well.

00:09:36.950 --> 00:09:41.630
So a few other details.

00:09:41.630 --> 00:09:45.320
So images come in all
sorts of resolutions.

00:09:45.320 --> 00:09:47.680
If you find an
internet image, it

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:52.460
might be long and
skinny or tall--

00:09:52.460 --> 00:09:52.960
sorry.

00:09:52.960 --> 00:09:55.000
Long and skinny
or the other way.

00:09:55.000 --> 00:09:57.240
So arbitrary dimensions.

00:09:57.240 --> 00:10:01.800
And one thing that you
learn about neural nets

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:03.820
is that they don't like
things to be dynamic.

00:10:03.820 --> 00:10:06.840
They want things
to be fixed size.

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:11.560
So there is a somewhat
heuristic processing

00:10:11.560 --> 00:10:16.600
that happens is they resize the
image so that the shorter side

00:10:16.600 --> 00:10:20.000
is 336 or whatever target size.

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:23.000
It could be 224 as well.

00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:27.520
And then they center
crop to get a square.

00:10:27.520 --> 00:10:29.660
OK, this is obviously
for convenience.

00:10:29.660 --> 00:10:32.580
Later, we'll see that you
can do better than this.

00:10:32.580 --> 00:10:35.000
But this is just for expediency.

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:39.560
And also at this
time the CLIP authors

00:10:39.560 --> 00:10:42.400
were thinking about
ImageNet and classification.

00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:46.140
So usually, the object
is in the middle

00:10:46.140 --> 00:10:48.040
and you're just trimming
off some background.

00:10:48.040 --> 00:10:49.760
So it doesn't matter too much.

00:10:55.840 --> 00:10:57.720
So what is the vision encoder?

00:10:57.720 --> 00:10:59.520
So this is the
general algorithm.

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:05.520
But I haven't specified what
the image and text encoders are.

00:11:05.520 --> 00:11:06.020
Yeah.

00:11:06.020 --> 00:11:24.700
[INAUDIBLE]

00:11:24.700 --> 00:11:28.100
So the question I think is why
they train on image-text pairs

00:11:28.100 --> 00:11:29.560
as opposed to images.

00:11:29.560 --> 00:11:33.660
[INAUDIBLE]

00:11:33.660 --> 00:11:36.380
So there is a line
of work that tries

00:11:36.380 --> 00:11:41.110
to encode images
into representations

00:11:41.110 --> 00:11:43.650
based on data augmentation.

00:11:43.650 --> 00:11:46.830
So, for example, if you
take an image-- and I think

00:11:46.830 --> 00:11:48.090
this idea called SimCLR.

00:11:48.090 --> 00:11:49.590
You augment it.

00:11:49.590 --> 00:11:50.410
You crop it.

00:11:50.410 --> 00:11:52.630
You randomly perturb it.

00:11:52.630 --> 00:11:53.570
Rotate it.

00:11:53.570 --> 00:11:56.850
And you want to say that
that is the same image.

00:11:56.850 --> 00:11:58.610
They should have
similar embeddings.

00:12:01.496 --> 00:12:05.410
And that's useful for
low level details.

00:12:05.410 --> 00:12:09.710
But the problem is
that you won't data

00:12:09.710 --> 00:12:14.390
augment your way from one
type of dog to another dog.

00:12:14.390 --> 00:12:16.910
So by using the
text, it gives you

00:12:16.910 --> 00:12:23.830
a higher level semantic
representations of images.

00:12:23.830 --> 00:12:25.930
And, yeah, that's
a good question.

00:12:25.930 --> 00:12:29.590
And later, we'll see how these
semantic representations are

00:12:29.590 --> 00:12:31.510
useful if you look
at the types of tasks

00:12:31.510 --> 00:12:33.850
that people are trying to solve.

00:12:36.870 --> 00:12:41.450
OK, so what is the vision
encoder that CLIP uses?

00:12:41.450 --> 00:12:44.930
They actually experimented
with a bunch of different ones,

00:12:44.930 --> 00:12:49.290
ResNets and vision transformers,
which had just come out.

00:12:49.290 --> 00:12:51.990
They found out that vision
transformers performed the best.

00:12:51.990 --> 00:12:56.770
So when people say CLIP, they
usually mean the VIT version.

00:12:56.770 --> 00:13:03.130
And so a vision transformer
does the following.

00:13:03.130 --> 00:13:10.930
So you take an image and you
break it up into these patches.

00:13:10.930 --> 00:13:15.250
I think the original
paper was 16 by 16

00:13:15.250 --> 00:13:18.850
and the CLIP used 14 by 14.

00:13:18.850 --> 00:13:21.730
But anyway, you have
these little patches,

00:13:21.730 --> 00:13:24.850
which are basically vectors.

00:13:24.850 --> 00:13:27.970
So each patches
in some sense a--

00:13:27.970 --> 00:13:31.930
well, a token for this
vision transformer.

00:13:31.930 --> 00:13:33.490
You add the
positional embeddings

00:13:33.490 --> 00:13:36.630
just like you would if you
were training a language model,

00:13:36.630 --> 00:13:42.310
and then you just put it
through a standard transformer.

00:13:42.310 --> 00:13:50.190
And if you want to
classify, then you would--

00:13:50.190 --> 00:13:53.750
well, you would
usually want to--

00:13:53.750 --> 00:13:55.230
OK, sorry.

00:13:55.230 --> 00:14:03.550
The transformer encoder
produces a sequence of vectors.

00:14:03.550 --> 00:14:07.030
And the CLIP paper does
something a little bit

00:14:07.030 --> 00:14:07.530
different.

00:14:07.530 --> 00:14:08.947
You can average
all these vectors.

00:14:08.947 --> 00:14:10.350
That gives you a single vector.

00:14:10.350 --> 00:14:14.230
But they thought it was better
to do attention pooling, which

00:14:14.230 --> 00:14:17.830
means that you take the global
average of all the activations,

00:14:17.830 --> 00:14:22.630
but then you also do
another round of attention

00:14:22.630 --> 00:14:27.590
with that query against the key
and values of each position.

00:14:27.590 --> 00:14:29.530
And that gives you
another vector,

00:14:29.530 --> 00:14:32.310
which is maybe a little
bit more informed

00:14:32.310 --> 00:14:37.080
rather than just a
straight up average.

00:14:37.080 --> 00:14:40.280
OK, so that's the VIT.

00:14:40.280 --> 00:14:45.720
And the CLIP paper found that
the best model is what is called

00:14:45.720 --> 00:14:46.320
VIT-L/14.

00:14:49.360 --> 00:14:54.040
So to decipher,
this is a large VIT.

00:14:54.040 --> 00:14:58.760
I think it has 24 layers or so.

00:14:58.760 --> 00:15:00.880
Not quite sure about that.

00:15:00.880 --> 00:15:03.960
And 14 by 14 patches.

00:15:03.960 --> 00:15:07.320
Each patch is RGB, so
it has three channels.

00:15:07.320 --> 00:15:13.420
And they trained on 336
by 336 resolution images.

00:15:13.420 --> 00:15:19.120
Actually, they
trained on a lower res

00:15:19.120 --> 00:15:23.452
I guess for speed because high
resolution images take longer.

00:15:23.452 --> 00:15:25.160
And then for the latter
part of training,

00:15:25.160 --> 00:15:27.720
they train on higher res.

00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:29.000
Yeah.

00:15:29.000 --> 00:15:31.040
For the position
embedding, would it

00:15:31.040 --> 00:15:33.440
be more complicated
than the task?

00:15:33.440 --> 00:15:36.140
For example, task
only has [INAUDIBLE].

00:15:40.660 --> 00:15:44.670
Yeah, so the
question is, can you

00:15:44.670 --> 00:15:46.420
be smarter about the
positional embeddings

00:15:46.420 --> 00:15:51.060
because this is just
linear 0 through 9?

00:15:51.060 --> 00:15:54.220
I think in this paper, they
actually-- in the CLIP paper,

00:15:54.220 --> 00:15:57.500
I believe they tried
some 2D version of this.

00:15:57.500 --> 00:16:02.300
And the one DNA found that it
doesn't really matter that much.

00:16:02.300 --> 00:16:05.440
I think it's also--

00:16:05.440 --> 00:16:09.260
I always have to take
these results with the idea

00:16:09.260 --> 00:16:11.480
that they're interested
in classification in mind.

00:16:11.480 --> 00:16:13.560
So for classification,
maybe it doesn't matter.

00:16:13.560 --> 00:16:16.540
Later, we'll see a fancier
positional embeddings

00:16:16.540 --> 00:16:20.840
that do take into account
the spatial structure.

00:16:24.740 --> 00:16:30.200
OK, so the text encoder
is a standard transformer.

00:16:30.200 --> 00:16:33.270
It's a GPT-2 style
transformer since that

00:16:33.270 --> 00:16:37.070
was from the same group
that developed GPT-2.

00:16:37.070 --> 00:16:42.830
And in order to get a single
vector out of a sequence,

00:16:42.830 --> 00:16:47.230
they prepended BOS
and appended EOS.

00:16:47.230 --> 00:16:51.390
And then they took EOS, the
activation at the highest layer

00:16:51.390 --> 00:16:57.830
as the representation
of that whole sequence.

00:16:57.830 --> 00:16:59.290
OK, so you have
the vision encoder.

00:16:59.290 --> 00:17:00.830
You have the text encoder.

00:17:00.830 --> 00:17:06.349
And then you can do
this process to train.

00:17:06.349 --> 00:17:07.369
You pick a batch.

00:17:07.369 --> 00:17:08.650
You encode all the text.

00:17:08.650 --> 00:17:10.250
You decode all the images.

00:17:10.250 --> 00:17:14.990
And then you form these two
end cross-entropy losses.

00:17:14.990 --> 00:17:18.270
And then you go from there.

00:17:18.270 --> 00:17:21.710
OK, so the headline
result, which

00:17:21.710 --> 00:17:25.910
got a lot of people excited
about CLIP back in 2021,

00:17:25.910 --> 00:17:28.690
is that on the
ImageNet benchmark,

00:17:28.690 --> 00:17:32.330
0 shot CLIP outperform a
ResNet that was trained

00:17:32.330 --> 00:17:36.090
on 1.2 million ImageNet images.

00:17:36.090 --> 00:17:39.270
So if you think about
this, 1.2 ImageNet images,

00:17:39.270 --> 00:17:44.650
this was many, many hours of
Amazon Mechanical Turk worker

00:17:44.650 --> 00:17:48.490
time to do this annotation.

00:17:48.490 --> 00:17:52.890
And now you have CLIP that was
trained on more organic web

00:17:52.890 --> 00:17:54.410
data.

00:17:54.410 --> 00:17:57.030
Of course, this was the labor
of a bunch of people on the web.

00:17:57.030 --> 00:18:01.730
But if you're able to leverage
that or existing data already,

00:18:01.730 --> 00:18:04.650
then you can just zero shot it.

00:18:04.650 --> 00:18:07.770
And the zero shot
technique is basically

00:18:07.770 --> 00:18:10.870
to explain I don't have
slide support here.

00:18:10.870 --> 00:18:14.230
But you take an image and
then you have a bunch of text.

00:18:14.230 --> 00:18:16.130
And then you basically
take the dot product

00:18:16.130 --> 00:18:19.790
with the various labels and
see which one's the highest.

00:18:23.630 --> 00:18:24.130
Oh, yeah.

00:18:24.130 --> 00:18:24.710
It is.

00:18:24.710 --> 00:18:25.250
Sorry.

00:18:25.250 --> 00:18:26.930
It is on this part of the slide.

00:18:26.930 --> 00:18:28.970
OK, good point.

00:18:28.970 --> 00:18:32.850
So this is a zero
shot prediction,

00:18:32.850 --> 00:18:35.450
where you take an image and
then you-- yeah, what I said.

00:18:40.870 --> 00:18:41.410
Cool.

00:18:41.410 --> 00:18:43.370
Any questions?

00:18:43.370 --> 00:18:43.870
Yeah.

00:18:43.870 --> 00:19:00.470
[INAUDIBLE]

00:19:00.470 --> 00:19:02.230
Yeah, so the question
is if you have a dog

00:19:02.230 --> 00:19:05.270
and there's maybe other
captions that also have dogs,

00:19:05.270 --> 00:19:08.670
would that confuse the model?

00:19:08.670 --> 00:19:13.430
In general, this process
is going to be noisy.

00:19:13.430 --> 00:19:18.172
It's OK if there's another
dog because, on average, it's

00:19:18.172 --> 00:19:20.130
unlikely that there's
always going to be a dog.

00:19:20.130 --> 00:19:23.950
Sometimes it's going to be an
apple or a cat or something.

00:19:23.950 --> 00:19:29.040
But also another point is that
these images are basically

00:19:29.040 --> 00:19:34.060
scraped from images on the web
with textual text next to them

00:19:34.060 --> 00:19:36.860
or in the alt image.

00:19:36.860 --> 00:19:38.900
And so they're extremely noisy.

00:19:38.900 --> 00:19:41.280
There's been studies
that look at when

00:19:41.280 --> 00:19:45.160
you have a caption of an image,
it doesn't necessarily just

00:19:45.160 --> 00:19:48.547
verbatim say what's
in the image.

00:19:48.547 --> 00:19:50.380
Because if you have an
image and it's a dog,

00:19:50.380 --> 00:19:52.760
you don't need to say a dog.

00:19:52.760 --> 00:19:55.220
So this process is very noisy.

00:19:55.220 --> 00:20:02.517
So it's maybe somewhat
surprising but also interesting

00:20:02.517 --> 00:20:03.100
that it works.

00:20:03.100 --> 00:20:06.060
I mean, there was a lot of
data filtering that is needed.

00:20:06.060 --> 00:20:09.300
So if you take arbitrary
images on and text on that web,

00:20:09.300 --> 00:20:12.920
it's probably going to
be too way too noisy.

00:20:12.920 --> 00:20:15.840
One other thing to
note, which relates

00:20:15.840 --> 00:20:18.120
to a point I'll come
back to at the very end,

00:20:18.120 --> 00:20:21.560
is they also try this
alternative where

00:20:21.560 --> 00:20:24.180
instead of doing this
ranking, they can

00:20:24.180 --> 00:20:26.520
predict a text from the images.

00:20:26.520 --> 00:20:28.100
So they can set
an objective where

00:20:28.100 --> 00:20:30.180
they try to take the
images and predict

00:20:30.180 --> 00:20:38.980
the text, either as a bag of
words or as a language model.

00:20:38.980 --> 00:20:43.780
And they show that maybe,
I don't know, somewhat

00:20:43.780 --> 00:20:47.080
surprisingly, that if
you use a stronger model,

00:20:47.080 --> 00:20:49.500
it actually does
worse or at least

00:20:49.500 --> 00:20:55.980
is less efficient compared
to using bag of words, CLIP.

00:20:55.980 --> 00:21:00.620
So this is saying that--

00:21:00.620 --> 00:21:05.420
I think saying that for
actually the representations

00:21:05.420 --> 00:21:08.020
that you're trying to
learn-- or, again, this

00:21:08.020 --> 00:21:09.120
is ImageNet accuracy.

00:21:09.120 --> 00:21:10.900
So it's classification.

00:21:10.900 --> 00:21:15.180
Actually modeling the exact
token sequences of the caption

00:21:15.180 --> 00:21:20.340
isn't so important for getting
the rough representation

00:21:20.340 --> 00:21:21.200
of the image.

00:21:24.484 --> 00:21:29.670
OK, we'll come back to
this point at the end.

00:21:29.670 --> 00:21:32.430
So what did we learned so far?

00:21:32.430 --> 00:21:35.550
So we've encoded
images using CLIP.

00:21:35.550 --> 00:21:38.990
And it captures the
semantics of the image

00:21:38.990 --> 00:21:40.650
because it's paired with text.

00:21:40.650 --> 00:21:44.830
And generally, text talks
about the semantics.

00:21:44.830 --> 00:21:48.030
It is important to note that
the design decisions here are

00:21:48.030 --> 00:21:50.190
based on image classification.

00:21:50.190 --> 00:21:53.150
So it's not very fine-grained.

00:21:53.150 --> 00:21:56.430
But yet, it's still,
as we'll see, serves

00:21:56.430 --> 00:21:58.990
as a robust starting
point for everything

00:21:58.990 --> 00:22:01.350
we're going to do later.

00:22:01.350 --> 00:22:07.750
One technical downside of CLIP
is that it does require large

00:22:07.750 --> 00:22:10.510
batch sizes, like 30,000.

00:22:10.510 --> 00:22:12.350
If you have a batch
size of 1, clearly, it

00:22:12.350 --> 00:22:14.830
doesn't work, or even
10, it doesn't work.

00:22:14.830 --> 00:22:17.590
And furthermore, the
softmax operation

00:22:17.590 --> 00:22:20.290
operates over the full batch.

00:22:20.290 --> 00:22:22.970
So it's not really
very decomposable.

00:22:22.970 --> 00:22:25.090
Whereas if you think about
normal language model

00:22:25.090 --> 00:22:30.250
training, a batch of examples,
all the sequences parallelize

00:22:30.250 --> 00:22:37.810
and you just do
aggregation at the end.

00:22:37.810 --> 00:22:42.930
So this point will be addressed
by another paper from Google

00:22:42.930 --> 00:22:45.930
that came out called SigLIP.

00:22:45.930 --> 00:22:49.770
And so think about this as
basically an improved version

00:22:49.770 --> 00:22:50.430
of CLIP.

00:22:50.430 --> 00:22:56.490
So it stands for Sigmoid Loss
for Language Image Pre-training.

00:22:56.490 --> 00:23:00.890
And here, the main
difference is that CLIP does

00:23:00.890 --> 00:23:02.830
multi-class classification.

00:23:02.830 --> 00:23:05.250
It says, I have this
aligned text and image.

00:23:05.250 --> 00:23:09.170
And I'm going to say this
is positive against all

00:23:09.170 --> 00:23:11.890
the other alternative
images or against all

00:23:11.890 --> 00:23:13.930
the alternative text.

00:23:13.930 --> 00:23:16.270
SigLIP, in some sense,
is a lot simpler.

00:23:16.270 --> 00:23:21.070
It basically says for any
given image text pair,

00:23:21.070 --> 00:23:23.190
are they aligned or not?

00:23:23.190 --> 00:23:27.030
So if I reference
this diagram up here,

00:23:27.030 --> 00:23:28.870
it's basically
binary classification

00:23:28.870 --> 00:23:31.030
where the diagonals
are positive examples

00:23:31.030 --> 00:23:33.630
and the diagonal are
negative examples.

00:23:33.630 --> 00:23:36.070
That's it.

00:23:36.070 --> 00:23:39.190
So here's the algorithm.

00:23:39.190 --> 00:23:40.930
You take the embeddings.

00:23:40.930 --> 00:23:41.970
You normalize.

00:23:41.970 --> 00:23:44.230
You take the dot
product as usual.

00:23:44.230 --> 00:23:50.390
But now you're forming the
labels which are minus 1

00:23:50.390 --> 00:23:53.950
on the off-diagonal, and
diagonals you have one.

00:23:53.950 --> 00:24:00.030
And then you do log_sigmoid.

00:24:00.030 --> 00:24:02.050
So the objective is very simple.

00:24:02.050 --> 00:24:03.275
Yeah.

00:24:03.275 --> 00:24:17.320
[INAUDIBLE]

00:24:17.320 --> 00:24:19.000
Yeah, so the question
is, does this

00:24:19.000 --> 00:24:23.680
require any sophisticated
sampling strategies?

00:24:23.680 --> 00:24:26.120
At least in the
initial paper, they

00:24:26.120 --> 00:24:31.280
were just operating on literally
the same type of matrix.

00:24:31.280 --> 00:24:34.520
You could imagine I think you're
probably thinking in general

00:24:34.520 --> 00:24:37.840
for these contrastive methods.

00:24:37.840 --> 00:24:40.280
The negative examples
need to be-- sometimes

00:24:40.280 --> 00:24:42.500
you need to balance the
positives and the negatives,

00:24:42.500 --> 00:24:45.160
or the negatives need to
be like tight negatives

00:24:45.160 --> 00:24:46.700
so that you're not biased.

00:24:46.700 --> 00:24:49.180
But at least in the
initial version,

00:24:49.180 --> 00:24:54.280
this was fairly straightforward.

00:24:54.280 --> 00:25:01.903
So to speak about the data,
this was done at Google.

00:25:01.903 --> 00:25:02.820
This was the data set.

00:25:02.820 --> 00:25:06.280
They used another paper, which
was another language image

00:25:06.280 --> 00:25:11.880
model around 2022, which got
a WebLI data set with order

00:25:11.880 --> 00:25:14.660
billion image text pairs.

00:25:14.660 --> 00:25:16.480
They also did some extra work.

00:25:16.480 --> 00:25:18.400
For example, images
that have text in them.

00:25:18.400 --> 00:25:19.620
They did OCR.

00:25:19.620 --> 00:25:23.140
And that's another way
to form text image pairs

00:25:23.140 --> 00:25:24.640
and did some filtering.

00:25:24.640 --> 00:25:26.920
This was multilingual.

00:25:26.920 --> 00:25:33.780
The main point of this paper
was that it was much more

00:25:33.780 --> 00:25:36.460
efficient to train than CLIP.

00:25:36.460 --> 00:25:42.540
So CLIP was trained
10 days on 256 TPUv3,

00:25:42.540 --> 00:25:46.260
and CLIP was five days on 32v4s.

00:25:46.260 --> 00:25:48.140
And you might think,
oh, v4s actually

00:25:48.140 --> 00:25:49.700
should be faster than v3.

00:25:49.700 --> 00:25:53.140
In terms of flops per
second, they're actually not.

00:25:53.140 --> 00:25:56.140
They're better because you
can put more of them in a pod

00:25:56.140 --> 00:25:57.560
and the interconnect is better.

00:25:57.560 --> 00:26:04.500
But at this scale, it's
really actually not faster.

00:26:04.500 --> 00:26:09.660
Actually, 60%
slower or something.

00:26:09.660 --> 00:26:15.700
So the way that this is
faster-- and, of course,

00:26:15.700 --> 00:26:17.160
I think CLIP
probably did not try

00:26:17.160 --> 00:26:21.180
to optimize the code for
the maximum throughput.

00:26:21.180 --> 00:26:24.560
It was just let's get
this thing to work.

00:26:24.560 --> 00:26:30.640
But the SigLIP paper showed
that you can parallelize this

00:26:30.640 --> 00:26:31.420
as follows.

00:26:31.420 --> 00:26:36.500
So you have-- basically, if you
recall your systems lecture,

00:26:36.500 --> 00:26:41.400
so this is you can think
about it as DDP if you like,

00:26:41.400 --> 00:26:51.880
where each device stores a
subset of the image text pairs.

00:26:51.880 --> 00:26:55.780
However, there's interactions
between the examples,

00:26:55.780 --> 00:26:58.360
unlike in language model
training, where everything just

00:26:58.360 --> 00:26:59.440
factors.

00:26:59.440 --> 00:27:02.560
So in the first
pass, each device

00:27:02.560 --> 00:27:09.840
just computes all the losses
on whatever image text

00:27:09.840 --> 00:27:11.410
pairs it has locally.

00:27:15.296 --> 00:27:19.150
And then you send the text.

00:27:19.150 --> 00:27:24.570
So basically, device
1 gets T5 through T8.

00:27:24.570 --> 00:27:27.270
And now it's able to
compute the negatives here.

00:27:27.270 --> 00:27:30.110
And then it gets T9 through T12.

00:27:30.110 --> 00:27:35.290
So you rotate around until you
cover all the off-diagonal block

00:27:35.290 --> 00:27:38.210
entries.

00:27:38.210 --> 00:27:42.850
Another thing that
was nice about SigLIP

00:27:42.850 --> 00:27:45.610
is that you effectively
decoupling batch size

00:27:45.610 --> 00:27:48.250
from the loss.

00:27:48.250 --> 00:27:51.430
Whereas in CLIP, the loss
is tied to the batch size.

00:27:51.430 --> 00:27:54.930
If you change the batch size,
it's a different loss function.

00:27:54.930 --> 00:27:59.010
So they were able to experiment
with much smaller batch sizes

00:27:59.010 --> 00:28:00.890
less than 16K.

00:28:00.890 --> 00:28:03.690
And in that regime, it's
much better than CLIP

00:28:03.690 --> 00:28:05.210
because the loss
function, if you

00:28:05.210 --> 00:28:10.490
have too many too small a
batch size, it just degrades.

00:28:10.490 --> 00:28:16.450
Whereas for smaller batch size
for CLIP, you have more variance

00:28:16.450 --> 00:28:18.770
but it's the same
in expectation.

00:28:18.770 --> 00:28:21.030
It's the same loss.

00:28:21.030 --> 00:28:24.730
They also say, well, you can go
up to much larger batch sizes.

00:28:24.730 --> 00:28:26.390
But that doesn't really help.

00:28:26.390 --> 00:28:30.670
And we saw that critical
batch sizes at some limit.

00:28:30.670 --> 00:28:34.370
And 32K was essentially
their critical batch size.

00:28:37.070 --> 00:28:40.670
OK, so now we have
CLIP and SigLIP.

00:28:40.670 --> 00:28:44.790
These are image encoders that
take an image, in this version,

00:28:44.790 --> 00:28:49.510
just a fixed size image, 336
by 336, let's say, and maps it

00:28:49.510 --> 00:28:56.790
into a vector, OK, which
contains some semantics.

00:28:56.790 --> 00:29:03.470
So now let's start building
VLMs, Vision Language Models.

00:29:03.470 --> 00:29:08.000
And I'm going to talk about
two families of models, LLaVA

00:29:08.000 --> 00:29:09.920
and Qwen.

00:29:09.920 --> 00:29:13.830
The two are very similar
in the broad template.

00:29:13.830 --> 00:29:15.580
There are some details
that are different,

00:29:15.580 --> 00:29:18.800
which we'll go through,
and the basic idea

00:29:18.800 --> 00:29:20.760
is that we're going to
take these embeddings

00:29:20.760 --> 00:29:23.640
and then just inject them
into our language model.

00:29:23.640 --> 00:29:26.840
So this is going to be more
of a flavor of mid-training

00:29:26.840 --> 00:29:30.420
or post-training, where we
take an existing image encoder.

00:29:30.420 --> 00:29:33.040
We take an existing
LLM, and then we're

00:29:33.040 --> 00:29:37.360
going to stitch it together
rather than training something

00:29:37.360 --> 00:29:40.680
from scratch.

00:29:40.680 --> 00:29:46.680
So LLaVA paper came out in 2023.

00:29:46.680 --> 00:29:51.080
And this got people excited
because around this time,

00:29:51.080 --> 00:29:55.760
the closed models, like
GPT, I think it was GPT-4,

00:29:55.760 --> 00:29:58.480
was able to do visual reasoning.

00:29:58.480 --> 00:30:02.240
And they were able to
show that they could do

00:30:02.240 --> 00:30:03.660
some visual reasoning as well.

00:30:03.660 --> 00:30:07.320
It wasn't as good
as GPT-4, of course,

00:30:07.320 --> 00:30:09.380
but I think it was an
open model and people

00:30:09.380 --> 00:30:12.260
got to see what
went under the hood.

00:30:12.260 --> 00:30:19.780
So for VLMs, there's
essentially a few pieces here.

00:30:19.780 --> 00:30:21.340
There's the vision encoder.

00:30:21.340 --> 00:30:23.260
They use CLIP here.

00:30:23.260 --> 00:30:26.740
For the text decoder, they use
a language model called Vicuna,

00:30:26.740 --> 00:30:30.500
which is the first Llama
model that was fine-tuned

00:30:30.500 --> 00:30:33.040
on some shared
GPT conversations.

00:30:33.040 --> 00:30:36.740
So these are conversations
that people have with ChatGPT

00:30:36.740 --> 00:30:38.400
and share them on this website.

00:30:38.400 --> 00:30:42.340
I don't think this
is up anymore.

00:30:42.340 --> 00:30:50.080
So the data that they
used was based on MS COCO.

00:30:50.080 --> 00:30:51.700
So this was an
annotated data set

00:30:51.700 --> 00:30:55.300
where they had Mechanical
Turk workers go and annotate

00:30:55.300 --> 00:30:59.020
a bunch of images with bounding
boxes and captions describing

00:30:59.020 --> 00:31:01.260
what was in the image.

00:31:01.260 --> 00:31:07.020
So they basically synthesized
a data set for their model.

00:31:07.020 --> 00:31:11.600
So they prompted GPT-4 with the
captions or the detected objects

00:31:11.600 --> 00:31:15.800
and GPT-4 to generate
questions or conversations.

00:31:15.800 --> 00:31:20.800
For example, this is an image
in the MS COCO data set.

00:31:20.800 --> 00:31:24.660
This was an annotated
caption done by a human.

00:31:24.660 --> 00:31:31.120
And also here is also
annotated by a human.

00:31:31.120 --> 00:31:37.120
And then you ask GPT-4,
generate me a conversation

00:31:37.120 --> 00:31:41.080
and you might get a
question and an answer.

00:31:41.080 --> 00:31:44.780
You might ask GPT-4 generate
me a detailed description.

00:31:44.780 --> 00:31:47.660
It generates something, I
guess, similar to the caption.

00:31:47.660 --> 00:31:50.980
And then you can ask GPT-4 to
generate complex reasoning.

00:31:50.980 --> 00:31:54.532
And it does something like that.

00:31:54.532 --> 00:32:03.390
OK, so those are synthesized
data, 158,000 examples.

00:32:03.390 --> 00:32:06.310
And then they trained
a model on this.

00:32:06.310 --> 00:32:12.610
So the model-- so they
use CLIP, in particular,

00:32:12.610 --> 00:32:18.090
the highest performing
CLIP version, VITL/14.

00:32:18.090 --> 00:32:21.770
So 14 by 14 patches.

00:32:21.770 --> 00:32:25.850
And then they-- let's
just look at this image.

00:32:25.850 --> 00:32:28.110
So they take images.

00:32:28.110 --> 00:32:30.410
They send it through
this vision encoder CLIP.

00:32:30.410 --> 00:32:32.410
That gives you a vector.

00:32:32.410 --> 00:32:35.990
But this vector isn't
really in the same space,

00:32:35.990 --> 00:32:38.770
so to speak, as the text.

00:32:38.770 --> 00:32:43.330
So then they multiply
by a matrix W

00:32:43.330 --> 00:32:47.890
to get another vector, which is
in the same space as the text

00:32:47.890 --> 00:32:49.010
embeddings.

00:32:49.010 --> 00:32:52.330
And for text, you basically just
use your standard embeddings

00:32:52.330 --> 00:32:54.850
to get vectors.

00:32:54.850 --> 00:32:58.810
So what's basically happening
is that the text gets encoded

00:32:58.810 --> 00:33:01.270
into these vectors and
the image also gets

00:33:01.270 --> 00:33:04.030
encoded into these vectors.

00:33:04.030 --> 00:33:06.670
And then this whole
sequence of vectors

00:33:06.670 --> 00:33:11.682
just goes through a standard
transformer and produces output.

00:33:11.682 --> 00:33:14.550
OK, so we're in some sense
converting these images

00:33:14.550 --> 00:33:18.750
into textual tokens.

00:33:18.750 --> 00:33:21.770
So I can leverage the
pre-trained language model.

00:33:24.510 --> 00:33:30.890
So to train this model,
they do it in two stages.

00:33:30.890 --> 00:33:33.670
The first stage is that
they freeze the vision

00:33:33.670 --> 00:33:39.070
encoder and the language model
and only train this matrix W.

00:33:39.070 --> 00:33:42.150
And this is what they call
the alignment phase, which

00:33:42.150 --> 00:33:46.830
is essentially making
sure that the image gets

00:33:46.830 --> 00:33:50.090
mapped into the same space.

00:33:50.090 --> 00:33:54.890
Because if you just
use a random W,

00:33:54.890 --> 00:33:58.520
these images are
not going to be--

00:33:58.520 --> 00:34:00.320
I mean, certainly,
they're not embeddings,

00:34:00.320 --> 00:34:03.760
representing any
natural language token.

00:34:03.760 --> 00:34:06.040
So the goal of
training W is that they

00:34:06.040 --> 00:34:09.840
look like natural
language token embeddings.

00:34:09.840 --> 00:34:12.840
And the second stage
is that they now

00:34:12.840 --> 00:34:15.000
still freeze a vision
encoder and then train

00:34:15.000 --> 00:34:16.219
W and the language model.

00:34:16.219 --> 00:34:22.520
So they're fine-tuning the
language model on their data.

00:34:22.520 --> 00:34:26.040
So remember, their data is
basically here's an image

00:34:26.040 --> 00:34:28.400
and here is a conversation
or a description

00:34:28.400 --> 00:34:32.080
or a complex reasoning example.

00:34:32.080 --> 00:34:37.960
So this is all image
plus text to text.

00:34:37.960 --> 00:34:43.440
OK, so this is one
example from their paper.

00:34:43.440 --> 00:34:44.659
It's interesting.

00:34:44.659 --> 00:34:48.543
So you have an input
image and a conversation.

00:34:48.543 --> 00:34:50.460
The user says what's
unusual about this image.

00:34:50.460 --> 00:34:54.239
And their model is able to
tell you that you don't usually

00:34:54.239 --> 00:34:57.380
iron on the back of a minivan.

00:34:57.380 --> 00:35:00.900
And they also make a point
that their model is pretty good

00:35:00.900 --> 00:35:06.940
because even if your
user prompt is not really

00:35:06.940 --> 00:35:12.180
prompting the unusualness, it
still talks about it, whereas--

00:35:12.180 --> 00:35:14.520
GPT-4, obviously,
is able to do this,

00:35:14.520 --> 00:35:20.650
but other models at the
time were not able to.

00:35:20.650 --> 00:35:25.200
OK, any questions about LLaVA?

00:35:25.200 --> 00:35:29.100
OK, so that was 2023.

00:35:29.100 --> 00:35:35.800
And I'm going to skip forward to
LLaVA OneVision, which is 2024.

00:35:35.800 --> 00:35:39.340
There is a sequence of papers
after the LLaVA paper came out

00:35:39.340 --> 00:35:44.220
of LLaVA 1.5, LLaVA-Next.

00:35:44.220 --> 00:35:47.080
But I'll capture all of
these innovations, I think,

00:35:47.080 --> 00:35:51.100
in what I'll describe next.

00:35:51.100 --> 00:35:55.000
The main thing that they
did is it's essentially

00:35:55.000 --> 00:35:56.960
the same recipe,
but they are trying

00:35:56.960 --> 00:35:59.120
to be more ambitious
in terms of the types

00:35:59.120 --> 00:36:02.880
of multimodal applications
they could do.

00:36:02.880 --> 00:36:06.680
So they were able to handle
multiple images and videos.

00:36:06.680 --> 00:36:10.060
So here's the same diagram.

00:36:10.060 --> 00:36:15.400
Now they can take a single
image or multiple images

00:36:15.400 --> 00:36:19.520
or a video, which is
essentially a sequence of images

00:36:19.520 --> 00:36:23.920
corresponding to a
sampling of the frames.

00:36:23.920 --> 00:36:26.060
And here are the vision encoder.

00:36:26.060 --> 00:36:28.920
They upgrade it to SigLIP.

00:36:28.920 --> 00:36:31.980
The text decoder,
they use Qwen-2 now,

00:36:31.980 --> 00:36:36.440
which was probably the
best language model

00:36:36.440 --> 00:36:38.400
that was out at that time.

00:36:38.400 --> 00:36:42.160
And then for the projector
or the adapter, which

00:36:42.160 --> 00:36:46.200
is the thing that takes the
output of the vision encoder

00:36:46.200 --> 00:36:49.680
and turns it into the
input of the language model

00:36:49.680 --> 00:36:55.170
is a two-layer MLP instead
of a linear projection.

00:36:55.170 --> 00:36:58.730
OK, so it's like
you have a system

00:36:58.730 --> 00:37:01.090
and you're just
upgrading the parts.

00:37:01.090 --> 00:37:05.370
But it's the same rough system.

00:37:05.370 --> 00:37:10.210
So let's talk a little
bit about data processing.

00:37:10.210 --> 00:37:14.290
So one thing that they
wanted to do is to do OCR.

00:37:14.290 --> 00:37:16.490
And the thing with
OCR is that you

00:37:16.490 --> 00:37:20.130
need to preserve very
fine-grained information.

00:37:20.130 --> 00:37:24.730
Otherwise, a J looks like
an I, and that's not good.

00:37:24.730 --> 00:37:30.170
So remember that CLIP resizes
and crops to 336 by 336.

00:37:30.170 --> 00:37:32.930
And clearly, if
you have a document

00:37:32.930 --> 00:37:36.690
and you crop to 336 by
336, you can't read it.

00:37:36.690 --> 00:37:40.730
So their solution
is this idea called

00:37:40.730 --> 00:37:45.010
AnyRes, which was actually
introduced in the LLaVA 1.5

00:37:45.010 --> 00:37:46.210
paper.

00:37:46.210 --> 00:37:49.870
And the idea is
basically, I mean,

00:37:49.870 --> 00:37:51.150
it's fairly straightforward.

00:37:51.150 --> 00:37:56.830
You break up an image
into multiple pieces.

00:37:56.830 --> 00:38:00.790
And the idea is that each piece
is the resolution of whatever

00:38:00.790 --> 00:38:05.590
the vision encoder is going to
take, 336 by 336, for example.

00:38:05.590 --> 00:38:09.890
So you're going to code all the
pieces into a bunch of vectors.

00:38:09.890 --> 00:38:12.230
And then you
concatenate the vectors.

00:38:12.230 --> 00:38:15.950
OK, so you're basically noticing
that your vision encoder

00:38:15.950 --> 00:38:18.310
can't handle high resolution.

00:38:18.310 --> 00:38:20.710
So rather than
downsampling, you're

00:38:20.710 --> 00:38:25.590
just going to crop and look
at different parts of image.

00:38:25.590 --> 00:38:29.710
And now if you have really super
high resolution images or videos

00:38:29.710 --> 00:38:31.330
then, you get too many tokens.

00:38:31.330 --> 00:38:35.230
So then they downsample.

00:38:35.230 --> 00:38:38.990
But the idea behind AnyRes
is that it's adaptive.

00:38:38.990 --> 00:38:43.130
And this is nice because the
transformer is already adaptive.

00:38:43.130 --> 00:38:46.230
Language can be-- your
sentences can be any length

00:38:46.230 --> 00:38:48.750
and the transformer already
handles that pretty well.

00:38:48.750 --> 00:38:51.660
So it turns out images
can be any resolution.

00:38:51.660 --> 00:38:58.040
So that's picking back on
the same dynamic ability.

00:38:58.040 --> 00:39:02.920
So here is what they
actually do for images here.

00:39:02.920 --> 00:39:07.360
So let's say you have
this image of a paper.

00:39:07.360 --> 00:39:11.560
They do downsample
and have one path,

00:39:11.560 --> 00:39:15.480
which is basically a downsampled
encoding of the entire paper.

00:39:15.480 --> 00:39:17.760
But then they break it
up into these chunks,

00:39:17.760 --> 00:39:19.560
where each chunk
is the resolution

00:39:19.560 --> 00:39:23.040
that the vision encoder expects,
and then encodes each of these

00:39:23.040 --> 00:39:24.160
separately.

00:39:24.160 --> 00:39:26.860
And then you concatenate
all these patches.

00:39:26.860 --> 00:39:28.760
And if you have
too many, then you

00:39:28.760 --> 00:39:33.740
reduce that the number of
patches by interpolation.

00:39:36.800 --> 00:39:42.080
OK, so then they're able to
handle images, multiple images,

00:39:42.080 --> 00:39:43.560
and video.

00:39:43.560 --> 00:39:46.800
So you think about,
well, all of these

00:39:46.800 --> 00:39:49.940
are technically all
reducible to images,

00:39:49.940 --> 00:39:52.300
but they put their thumb
on the scale a little bit

00:39:52.300 --> 00:39:59.260
because they want to make sure
all the modalities are roughly

00:39:59.260 --> 00:40:00.638
comparable.

00:40:00.638 --> 00:40:02.180
Because videos can
be very long, they

00:40:02.180 --> 00:40:04.260
don't want their data
set to be dominated

00:40:04.260 --> 00:40:07.180
by a bunch of repetitive frames.

00:40:07.180 --> 00:40:09.420
So here's what they do here.

00:40:09.420 --> 00:40:11.980
So for a single
image, they basically

00:40:11.980 --> 00:40:14.900
have the full image
downsampled and then

00:40:14.900 --> 00:40:20.260
a bunch of crops up to
N crops there, up to 9.

00:40:20.260 --> 00:40:23.460
And if you are presented
with multiple images,

00:40:23.460 --> 00:40:28.880
then they just use
the base resolution.

00:40:28.880 --> 00:40:30.380
So if you have a
single image, I get

00:40:30.380 --> 00:40:31.882
to look at it more carefully.

00:40:31.882 --> 00:40:33.340
If I have multiple
images, I'm just

00:40:33.340 --> 00:40:37.780
going to look at it from afar.

00:40:37.780 --> 00:40:43.100
And for video, we're going
to use even lower resolution

00:40:43.100 --> 00:40:46.920
or fewer tokens to represent
each frame because the idea is

00:40:46.920 --> 00:40:54.160
that, well, videos can be--
they only use up to 32 frames,

00:40:54.160 --> 00:40:58.320
but you start running
into context length

00:40:58.320 --> 00:41:01.280
problems for viewing.

00:41:01.280 --> 00:41:04.760
And later, we'll
see how a big part

00:41:04.760 --> 00:41:06.960
of being able to
handle multimodal

00:41:06.960 --> 00:41:09.220
is to deal with longer context.

00:41:11.960 --> 00:41:22.400
OK, so the data here that
they use is, I would say,

00:41:22.400 --> 00:41:26.120
the state of philosophy is they
want to curate high quality data

00:41:26.120 --> 00:41:27.120
and quantity.

00:41:27.120 --> 00:41:31.020
Another way to interpret this
is that it's very targeted data.

00:41:31.020 --> 00:41:34.860
If you look at many of
these, it's very task-based

00:41:34.860 --> 00:41:40.040
whether you're doing visual
question answering or answering

00:41:40.040 --> 00:41:41.760
questions about tables.

00:41:41.760 --> 00:41:45.570
So this is definitely
post-training territory,

00:41:45.570 --> 00:41:49.850
where you're trying to get
your model to do these tasks,

00:41:49.850 --> 00:41:52.210
and therefore, you create
a bunch of these tasks.

00:41:52.210 --> 00:41:57.970
And this work is also
unabashedly, basically,

00:41:57.970 --> 00:42:01.890
distilling GPT-4
models so that they

00:42:01.890 --> 00:42:07.070
can get the best performance,
which is, I guess, not ideal.

00:42:07.070 --> 00:42:14.530
But this is what you do if you
don't have an annotation budget.

00:42:14.530 --> 00:42:20.390
And so they have single images,
multiple images, and videos.

00:42:20.390 --> 00:42:24.870
So there's a lot of
different pieces.

00:42:24.870 --> 00:42:26.630
Some of these are very specific.

00:42:26.630 --> 00:42:31.050
You give two images and you're
trying to spot the difference.

00:42:31.050 --> 00:42:33.050
But one thing that--

00:42:33.050 --> 00:42:36.470
OK, I'll come back to
this at this point later.

00:42:36.470 --> 00:42:42.550
So for training, it's roughly
the same idea as before,

00:42:42.550 --> 00:42:48.190
where the first stage is that
you're focused on alignment.

00:42:48.190 --> 00:42:54.830
So in particular, you
only train the projector,

00:42:54.830 --> 00:42:58.510
which is the adapter.

00:42:58.510 --> 00:43:02.990
And then in the second
stage and the third stages

00:43:02.990 --> 00:43:04.710
you train the full model.

00:43:04.710 --> 00:43:06.170
And before, we had two stages.

00:43:06.170 --> 00:43:09.950
Now, we have three stages.

00:43:09.950 --> 00:43:14.390
I'm not sure there's any
particular principled reason

00:43:14.390 --> 00:43:16.230
for this, except for
this second stage is

00:43:16.230 --> 00:43:19.810
trying to put high quality data
but more focused on knowledge.

00:43:19.810 --> 00:43:24.110
And the final stage is
focusing on examples that look

00:43:24.110 --> 00:43:25.890
like your downstream tasks.

00:43:30.230 --> 00:43:34.030
OK, so one thing that they
found in this paper, which

00:43:34.030 --> 00:43:41.010
was interesting, is that you
get transfer between modalities.

00:43:41.010 --> 00:43:44.570
So even though they have
only single image data

00:43:44.570 --> 00:43:47.370
for diagrams and charts,
but this actually

00:43:47.370 --> 00:43:49.070
generalizes to multiple images.

00:43:49.070 --> 00:43:54.130
So at training time,
you never saw an example

00:43:54.130 --> 00:43:56.168
where you have a
table and a chart

00:43:56.168 --> 00:43:57.710
and you're asking
questions about it.

00:43:57.710 --> 00:44:04.130
But at test time, you have
two images, one for the table

00:44:04.130 --> 00:44:05.270
and one for the chart.

00:44:05.270 --> 00:44:09.650
And you're able to have some
of conversation about it.

00:44:09.650 --> 00:44:14.290
At training time, you only have
OCR data on single image data,

00:44:14.290 --> 00:44:18.850
and you only have relational
reasoning from multiple images.

00:44:18.850 --> 00:44:23.010
But you can generalize
to these cases which

00:44:23.010 --> 00:44:25.430
are useful for
powering GUI agents,

00:44:25.430 --> 00:44:28.150
where you show it a
bunch of screenshots.

00:44:28.150 --> 00:44:32.410
This is multiple images and
it can generate a description

00:44:32.410 --> 00:44:36.770
of the screenshots.

00:44:36.770 --> 00:44:39.220
Another example
is that they have

00:44:39.220 --> 00:44:42.460
this idea of visual
prompting, where

00:44:42.460 --> 00:44:45.640
you have this circle, which
is a part of the image,

00:44:45.640 --> 00:44:48.180
but that's meant to highlight
the part of the image

00:44:48.180 --> 00:44:51.860
that you're supposed
to do something about.

00:44:51.860 --> 00:44:54.660
And this type of data only
exists for single images.

00:44:54.660 --> 00:44:59.420
And now it can
generalize to videos

00:44:59.420 --> 00:45:02.620
where the user says describe the
player highlighted in the video.

00:45:02.620 --> 00:45:06.860
And this player is
across multiple frames.

00:45:06.860 --> 00:45:11.940
So when you first look
at this and say, oh, boy.

00:45:11.940 --> 00:45:14.120
You're basically targeting
each of these tasks.

00:45:14.120 --> 00:45:16.600
It's supervised learning.

00:45:16.600 --> 00:45:19.020
But if you have enough
tasks, these models

00:45:19.020 --> 00:45:26.560
seem to do some transfer,
which is, I guess, reassuring.

00:45:29.300 --> 00:45:33.660
OK, so again, this is
a fairly standard VLM

00:45:33.660 --> 00:45:37.680
template, vision encoder plus
the projector into the language

00:45:37.680 --> 00:45:39.800
model.

00:45:39.800 --> 00:45:43.540
There's a lot of work that
goes into the data curation.

00:45:43.540 --> 00:45:48.120
They lean pretty heavily into
synthesize task-specific data.

00:45:48.120 --> 00:45:49.960
The nice thing about
the LLaVA series

00:45:49.960 --> 00:45:53.780
is that it's one of the few
works that open sources.

00:45:53.780 --> 00:45:56.620
They're not just the model
weights, but also the data.

00:45:56.620 --> 00:46:01.120
So you can really replicate
and study this stuff.

00:46:01.120 --> 00:46:06.680
OK, now let's talk
about the Qwen Models.

00:46:06.680 --> 00:46:11.860
So Qwen started also training
in 2023 multimodal models.

00:46:11.860 --> 00:46:14.480
So the first
version was Qwen-VL.

00:46:14.480 --> 00:46:17.360
And hopefully, I'll
go through this

00:46:17.360 --> 00:46:19.780
quickly because you
hopefully see the pattern.

00:46:19.780 --> 00:46:23.560
The vision encoder,
they use Open-CLIP.

00:46:23.560 --> 00:46:27.960
Remember, OpenCLIP was the open
reproductions of the CLIP model.

00:46:27.960 --> 00:46:31.760
So it's basically
a CLIP encoder.

00:46:31.760 --> 00:46:39.250
And for the adapter, they did
one layer of cross-attention,

00:46:39.250 --> 00:46:42.970
incorporate 2D positional
embeddings and maps to a fixed

00:46:42.970 --> 00:46:47.850
size of 256, which
is a little bit--

00:46:51.210 --> 00:46:54.090
I guess this will be changed
later because this is definitely

00:46:54.090 --> 00:46:56.730
not very dynamic, but
neither is the vision encoder

00:46:56.730 --> 00:46:58.490
at this point.

00:46:58.490 --> 00:47:00.740
I guess this got cut off.

00:47:00.740 --> 00:47:03.810
But the special
tokens, they have

00:47:03.810 --> 00:47:06.390
an image tag and a box
tag for bounding box,

00:47:06.390 --> 00:47:10.450
and a ref tag for descriptions.

00:47:10.450 --> 00:47:14.410
This is what happens when
your slides are in HTML.

00:47:14.410 --> 00:47:18.090
So for training, they
have three stages, just

00:47:18.090 --> 00:47:19.830
like with the LLaVA models.

00:47:19.830 --> 00:47:23.090
In the first stage,
the idea is to--

00:47:23.090 --> 00:47:26.930
I mean, they call it
pre-training but you're not

00:47:26.930 --> 00:47:29.490
pre-training from scratch.

00:47:29.490 --> 00:47:33.350
This is generally
large-scale, low quality data.

00:47:33.350 --> 00:47:38.150
They freeze the
language model and train

00:47:38.150 --> 00:47:41.070
the vision encoder and adapter.

00:47:41.070 --> 00:47:45.390
So this is a bit different
from the LLaVA models, in

00:47:45.390 --> 00:47:51.550
that, they train the vision
encoder at this point.

00:47:51.550 --> 00:47:55.830
And you can see some of the data
sets that they are training on,

00:47:55.830 --> 00:47:58.430
1.4 billion examples.

00:47:58.430 --> 00:48:02.770
Stage 2, they move to higher
quality task-specific data.

00:48:02.770 --> 00:48:08.550
And you can see some of the
usual suspects of VQA data sets,

00:48:08.550 --> 00:48:11.790
chart question
answering, and so on.

00:48:11.790 --> 00:48:13.850
And then they train all
the parameters here.

00:48:13.850 --> 00:48:19.790
And finally, stage 3 is
instruction tuning data.

00:48:19.790 --> 00:48:25.710
You freeze the visual encoder
and you train the adapter

00:48:25.710 --> 00:48:27.590
and the language model.

00:48:27.590 --> 00:48:29.670
Here are some examples
of the type of things

00:48:29.670 --> 00:48:30.890
that they're able to do.

00:48:30.890 --> 00:48:33.110
So, of course, this is Qwen.

00:48:33.110 --> 00:48:37.570
So some of these
examples are in Chinese.

00:48:37.570 --> 00:48:40.090
Their interest is
also making a model

00:48:40.090 --> 00:48:44.350
that is supposed to be
subsuming a language model.

00:48:44.350 --> 00:48:46.250
So a language model can do code.

00:48:46.250 --> 00:48:51.890
So we want the vision model
to be good at code as well.

00:48:51.890 --> 00:48:56.630
And you can have this example,
if you can see it, is an image.

00:48:56.630 --> 00:48:58.450
And can you find
Spider-Man and the Hulk.

00:48:58.450 --> 00:49:01.770
And it can output
bounding boxes as well.

00:49:01.770 --> 00:49:03.270
It doesn't actually
output an image.

00:49:03.270 --> 00:49:07.070
It just outputs a bounding box.

00:49:10.090 --> 00:49:11.730
And then you can do OCR.

00:49:11.730 --> 00:49:16.730
You can compare--
so usual stuff.

00:49:16.730 --> 00:49:22.150
OK, so then Qwen-2
was, again, a upgrade.

00:49:22.150 --> 00:49:25.650
There's some new ideas
here, which I'll go through.

00:49:25.650 --> 00:49:29.010
So use a larger vision encoder.

00:49:29.010 --> 00:49:32.900
They also started doing
dynamic resolution.

00:49:32.900 --> 00:49:34.540
I think this was the main thing.

00:49:34.540 --> 00:49:37.420
We remember when we went from
LLaVA to LLaVA OneVision,

00:49:37.420 --> 00:49:39.980
they realized, oh,
we need to handle

00:49:39.980 --> 00:49:42.440
images of different sizes.

00:49:42.440 --> 00:49:44.620
And if you try to
do video, it's clear

00:49:44.620 --> 00:49:47.700
that you need some
dynamic resolution.

00:49:47.700 --> 00:49:54.940
So the model is basically-- so
let's say you have this picture

00:49:54.940 --> 00:50:00.660
here, that might be
mapped to 11,000 tokens.

00:50:00.660 --> 00:50:06.140
This tiny picture of an equation
might be only mapped to 8 tokens

00:50:06.140 --> 00:50:09.380
and so on and so forth.

00:50:09.380 --> 00:50:13.220
So how do they do this?

00:50:13.220 --> 00:50:18.620
Well, basically each-- it's
the same idea AnyRes idea

00:50:18.620 --> 00:50:26.980
from LLaVA, where each 224 by
224 patch is encoded with a VIT.

00:50:26.980 --> 00:50:30.480
And remember the VIT
that they started

00:50:30.480 --> 00:50:32.920
with is the OpenCLIP bit.

00:50:32.920 --> 00:50:36.880
But this gets fine-tuned.

00:50:36.880 --> 00:50:40.060
And then trying to
reduce the contact size,

00:50:40.060 --> 00:50:43.200
they compress
every 2 by 2 into 1

00:50:43.200 --> 00:50:50.280
so that they ultimately edge
every patch generates 66 tokens.

00:50:50.280 --> 00:50:54.640
For eight videos, they sample
2 frames a second but max out

00:50:54.640 --> 00:50:59.600
at 16,000 tokens.

00:50:59.600 --> 00:51:01.800
OK, so one thing coming
back to this question

00:51:01.800 --> 00:51:05.840
about positional
embeddings, so they did

00:51:05.840 --> 00:51:09.360
do something different
here with Qwen-2.

00:51:09.360 --> 00:51:14.320
So they used this idea called
multimodal rotary, multimodal

00:51:14.320 --> 00:51:15.200
RoPE.

00:51:15.200 --> 00:51:17.640
So remember, we saw RoPE before.

00:51:17.640 --> 00:51:22.400
And the idea behind RoPE is
that you have embedding such

00:51:22.400 --> 00:51:26.930
that the inner product
between the vectors

00:51:26.930 --> 00:51:29.490
depends only on the distance.

00:51:29.490 --> 00:51:34.790
So the distance is defined in
1D by just the number of tokens.

00:51:34.790 --> 00:51:36.450
You're away from it.

00:51:36.450 --> 00:51:39.130
So now, the
multidimensional version

00:51:39.130 --> 00:51:41.030
is essentially the same thing.

00:51:41.030 --> 00:51:44.490
Except for now you have 3D.

00:51:44.490 --> 00:51:45.630
So you have height.

00:51:45.630 --> 00:51:47.690
You have width
and you have time.

00:51:47.690 --> 00:51:53.610
So each position, each
patch now is a triple

00:51:53.610 --> 00:51:56.050
defined by the coordinates.

00:51:56.050 --> 00:52:01.850
And then to compute the M-RoPE,
basically, for every dimension,

00:52:01.850 --> 00:52:04.610
you compute the RoPE and
then you concatenate.

00:52:04.610 --> 00:52:09.290
So it's a fairly
straightforward idea.

00:52:09.290 --> 00:52:11.610
Although, later,
with Qwen-3, we'll

00:52:11.610 --> 00:52:17.330
see how this is actually
a bit suboptimal.

00:52:17.330 --> 00:52:22.170
So they initialize the
language model with--

00:52:22.170 --> 00:52:23.790
this is the Qwen-VL model.

00:52:23.790 --> 00:52:26.510
So, of course, they're
initializing with Gwen.

00:52:26.510 --> 00:52:30.190
And then they have three
stages of training,

00:52:30.190 --> 00:52:34.670
which is very similar
to what we did before.

00:52:34.670 --> 00:52:37.150
And then they were
able to show a bunch

00:52:37.150 --> 00:52:41.990
of different capabilities,
some video understanding.

00:52:41.990 --> 00:52:46.790
It can do math and
code, function calling,

00:52:46.790 --> 00:52:50.590
and so on and so forth.

00:52:50.590 --> 00:52:53.310
OK, so quickly, I'll
talk about Gwen-3.

00:52:56.110 --> 00:52:59.950
So again, there's
a few changes here.

00:52:59.950 --> 00:53:03.010
I wouldn't say these are
structural big changes,

00:53:03.010 --> 00:53:05.830
but they're probably
changes that do impact

00:53:05.830 --> 00:53:07.210
the quality of the model.

00:53:07.210 --> 00:53:11.470
So this is from last
year Gwen3-VL report.

00:53:11.470 --> 00:53:16.710
And so it's roughly
the same diagram here.

00:53:16.710 --> 00:53:19.478
I'll point out maybe--

00:53:19.478 --> 00:53:20.770
I'll come back to this diagram.

00:53:20.770 --> 00:53:24.190
I'll go through
some of the changes.

00:53:24.190 --> 00:53:26.830
So one is that now they're
using Qwen-3 models.

00:53:26.830 --> 00:53:29.270
They have a series of
dense and MoE models.

00:53:29.270 --> 00:53:32.350
And the Qwen-3 models
are really, really good,

00:53:32.350 --> 00:53:35.890
and it really helps the
quality of the final model.

00:53:35.890 --> 00:53:39.430
One thing that they're vested in
is long context understanding.

00:53:39.430 --> 00:53:42.770
So their context length
can go up to 256K,

00:53:42.770 --> 00:53:48.290
which is really important if
you're trying to do long video.

00:53:48.290 --> 00:53:50.730
Let's talk about
the vision encoder.

00:53:50.730 --> 00:53:53.610
So they use SigLIP-2,
which is basically

00:53:53.610 --> 00:53:55.350
an improved version of SigLIP.

00:53:55.350 --> 00:53:56.970
It's actually
identical architecture,

00:53:56.970 --> 00:54:00.650
is designed to be
backward compatible.

00:54:00.650 --> 00:54:04.490
And then they
improved their RoPE.

00:54:04.490 --> 00:54:10.250
And the way to explain
this is, before, you

00:54:10.250 --> 00:54:13.490
had the three dimensions,
time, width, and height.

00:54:13.490 --> 00:54:20.330
And basically, when
to basically allocate

00:54:20.330 --> 00:54:24.460
the first block of
dimensions to the time,

00:54:24.460 --> 00:54:28.060
then the next block to width
and the next to height.

00:54:28.060 --> 00:54:31.320
And the problem, if
you remember RoPE

00:54:31.320 --> 00:54:34.740
is that each component
refers represents

00:54:34.740 --> 00:54:36.980
a different frequency.

00:54:36.980 --> 00:54:39.860
So this would mean that maybe
all the temporal dimensions

00:54:39.860 --> 00:54:42.220
are low frequency and
all the high dimensions

00:54:42.220 --> 00:54:43.940
are high frequency.

00:54:43.940 --> 00:54:48.920
So they basically interleave
them when they realize that.

00:54:48.920 --> 00:54:55.660
And they now all
the axes are exposed

00:54:55.660 --> 00:54:59.300
to both low and high frequency.

00:54:59.300 --> 00:55:04.740
Another thing they did was
explicit video timestamps.

00:55:04.740 --> 00:55:08.840
So if you look closely at this--

00:55:12.020 --> 00:55:12.900
wait.

00:55:12.900 --> 00:55:14.900
OK, this is Qwen-3.

00:55:14.900 --> 00:55:16.300
Oops.

00:55:16.300 --> 00:55:17.660
So if you look--

00:55:17.660 --> 00:55:19.580
this is probably a
little bit too small.

00:55:19.580 --> 00:55:25.000
But before, the time
stamp was implicit

00:55:25.000 --> 00:55:28.280
in the positional encodings.

00:55:28.280 --> 00:55:30.600
Each frame in the
video intrinsically

00:55:30.600 --> 00:55:33.920
gets a different--
there's a notion of time

00:55:33.920 --> 00:55:37.120
just because it's in
a different position.

00:55:37.120 --> 00:55:39.620
But here, they made
the time explicit.

00:55:39.620 --> 00:55:42.040
So these are actually
tokens zero seconds,

00:55:42.040 --> 00:55:45.640
is a token that is now
you have a token that

00:55:45.640 --> 00:55:47.880
represents the time.

00:55:47.880 --> 00:55:51.812
And they found this to be
helpful presumably because it's

00:55:51.812 --> 00:55:53.520
something that you
can directly refer to,

00:55:53.520 --> 00:55:56.300
like what happened
after two seconds.

00:55:59.080 --> 00:56:05.520
So that is one of
the main changes.

00:56:05.520 --> 00:56:10.440
They also did this square root
normalized per token loss.

00:56:10.440 --> 00:56:14.320
So the idea is that
some examples that

00:56:14.320 --> 00:56:18.260
involve video are very long
and some examples that involve

00:56:18.260 --> 00:56:20.460
a single image are very short.

00:56:20.460 --> 00:56:24.780
And the normal thing is that
every token is treated the same.

00:56:24.780 --> 00:56:26.940
And this would mean
that the video examples

00:56:26.940 --> 00:56:29.700
are going to dominate.

00:56:29.700 --> 00:56:33.000
So they basically-- the
details aren't too clear.

00:56:33.000 --> 00:56:35.860
But I believe they
normalized each example

00:56:35.860 --> 00:56:41.940
by the square root of the
length to basically downweight

00:56:41.940 --> 00:56:46.500
the impact of really
long examples.

00:56:46.500 --> 00:56:49.600
The final thing that is worth
noting, which is interesting,

00:56:49.600 --> 00:56:51.900
is that they did something
interesting with adapter.

00:56:51.900 --> 00:56:53.820
So remember we
started the adapter is

00:56:53.820 --> 00:56:56.260
the thing that connects the
vision encoder to the language

00:56:56.260 --> 00:56:56.980
model.

00:56:56.980 --> 00:56:58.407
And LLaVA was the simplest.

00:56:58.407 --> 00:56:59.740
It was just a linear projection.

00:56:59.740 --> 00:57:01.180
And then we got an MLP.

00:57:01.180 --> 00:57:03.100
And then we got some
cross-attention.

00:57:03.100 --> 00:57:08.060
And DeepStack, which is actually
a paper from the DeepSeek team,

00:57:08.060 --> 00:57:14.020
but when used here is
more sophisticated.

00:57:14.020 --> 00:57:18.030
They noticed that, well, the
vision encoder already computes

00:57:18.030 --> 00:57:23.350
a stack of vision embeddings.

00:57:23.350 --> 00:57:26.870
And we're basically
going to basically add

00:57:26.870 --> 00:57:34.790
these directly into the residual
stream of the language model.

00:57:34.790 --> 00:57:42.630
OK, so this is a bit more of
a deep fusion of the vision

00:57:42.630 --> 00:57:47.750
encoder into the language model
as opposed to the vision encoder

00:57:47.750 --> 00:57:51.410
is a black box that just
outputs a sequence of vectors.

00:57:54.430 --> 00:57:57.950
OK, so training is--

00:57:57.950 --> 00:58:00.390
so they have two phases.

00:58:00.390 --> 00:58:02.718
Pre-training has
actually four stages.

00:58:02.718 --> 00:58:04.010
Post-training has three stages.

00:58:04.010 --> 00:58:06.510
So you can see that
this pipelines are

00:58:06.510 --> 00:58:10.430
getting quite complicated now.

00:58:10.430 --> 00:58:11.890
So they first train the adapter.

00:58:11.890 --> 00:58:14.850
This is the same as
all of these models.

00:58:14.850 --> 00:58:18.170
And then they have three stages
where they progressively train

00:58:18.170 --> 00:58:25.290
on longer and longer sequences,
from 8K, to 32K, to 256K.

00:58:25.290 --> 00:58:32.090
Most of the tokens are
in the stages 2 and 3.

00:58:32.090 --> 00:58:34.530
And then for
post-training, they do

00:58:34.530 --> 00:58:39.430
SFT on long chain of thought
data, knowledge distillation,

00:58:39.430 --> 00:58:43.730
and then some
reinforcement learning.

00:58:43.730 --> 00:58:46.070
At this point, it's
really a systems paper.

00:58:46.070 --> 00:58:48.570
There's a bunch of--

00:58:48.570 --> 00:58:51.190
I highlighted the
core new ideas,

00:58:51.190 --> 00:58:54.610
but there's a lot of
details that are different.

00:58:54.610 --> 00:58:56.230
And if you look at
the final results,

00:58:56.230 --> 00:58:59.150
this is a pretty good model.

00:58:59.150 --> 00:59:01.690
I mean, if you look at
these benchmarks as compared

00:59:01.690 --> 00:59:09.136
to the closed models
Gemini, GPT-5, and Opus 4.1,

00:59:09.136 --> 00:59:11.090
these numbers are--

00:59:11.090 --> 00:59:15.140
the bold means that it's
the best number in that row.

00:59:15.140 --> 00:59:18.320
So the Qwen models are
actually quite strong.

00:59:21.340 --> 00:59:25.450
OK, maybe the--
yeah, a question.

00:59:25.450 --> 00:59:54.900
[INAUDIBLE]

00:59:54.900 --> 00:59:59.860
Yeah, so the question is
what about video generation

00:59:59.860 --> 01:00:02.480
and how do you know whether
there should be a video or text?

01:00:02.480 --> 01:00:07.260
So first of all, these models
don't generate videos or images.

01:00:07.260 --> 01:00:09.660
All the multimodal stuff
is on the input side.

01:00:09.660 --> 01:00:11.900
You're always generating text.

01:00:16.200 --> 01:00:21.760
And then you're always in most
of these stages, except for RL,

01:00:21.760 --> 01:00:24.040
you're supervising every token.

01:00:24.040 --> 01:00:31.040
So there's no LLM as a
judge or quality on how well

01:00:31.040 --> 01:00:32.220
the description is working.

01:00:32.220 --> 01:00:34.800
It's just basically whatever
is in your data set.

01:00:34.800 --> 01:00:39.520
Now, with RL, you can play
around with different rewards.

01:00:39.520 --> 01:00:41.040
Yeah.

01:00:41.040 --> 01:00:45.280
I'll ask is the training
of multimodal models

01:00:45.280 --> 01:00:51.780
harder than training a pure
LLM from a system perspective?

01:00:51.780 --> 01:01:16.860
[INAUDIBLE]

01:01:16.860 --> 01:01:22.500
Yeah, so the two questions are
trained multimodal models harder

01:01:22.500 --> 01:01:24.020
on the system side?

01:01:24.020 --> 01:01:27.820
And, I mean, certainly,
it's not easier.

01:01:27.820 --> 01:01:32.120
I think you pointed out that
the data sets are larger.

01:01:32.120 --> 01:01:36.000
Video data is just-- even
loading it can be a bottleneck.

01:01:36.000 --> 01:01:38.580
Whereas generally,
we have not really

01:01:38.580 --> 01:01:41.700
focused on data loading for
talking about language models

01:01:41.700 --> 01:01:44.600
because it's very, very cheap.

01:01:44.600 --> 01:01:47.360
So that needs to be
taken into account.

01:01:47.360 --> 01:01:49.740
And all the same
principles of making sure

01:01:49.740 --> 01:01:51.420
that your data
loading is happening

01:01:51.420 --> 01:01:56.300
async with actual computation
needs to be taken into account.

01:01:56.300 --> 01:01:59.300
And the second part was--

01:01:59.300 --> 01:02:01.200
sorry, what was the
second question?

01:02:01.200 --> 01:02:02.860
[INAUDIBLE]

01:02:02.860 --> 01:02:03.560
Oh, yeah.

01:02:03.560 --> 01:02:07.950
So the videos and images
can have more-- especially

01:02:07.950 --> 01:02:09.590
video have more tokens.

01:02:09.590 --> 01:02:14.270
So that's one reason we
saw this normalization

01:02:14.270 --> 01:02:19.192
to make sure that we're not
giving more weight to video.

01:02:19.192 --> 01:02:20.650
I mean, you can
always down weight.

01:02:20.650 --> 01:02:22.775
So if you have more tokens,
if you don't like that,

01:02:22.775 --> 01:02:24.110
you can down weight.

01:02:24.110 --> 01:02:29.110
We talked in the last or two
weeks ago about data mixtures.

01:02:29.110 --> 01:02:31.390
So you can always
weight things according

01:02:31.390 --> 01:02:35.750
to whatever makes sense.

01:02:35.750 --> 01:02:38.870
But there's also a lot
of text tokens out there.

01:02:38.870 --> 01:02:44.090
So I think, in general,
if you look at, yeah,

01:02:44.090 --> 01:02:48.550
the models are trained on
tens of trillions of tokens.

01:02:48.550 --> 01:02:50.310
I think there's a--
yeah, I wouldn't

01:02:50.310 --> 01:02:52.990
say that the number
of multimodal tokens

01:02:52.990 --> 01:02:56.470
vastly outnumbers text tokens.

01:02:56.470 --> 01:03:00.370
Yeah, oh, I think
you were first.

01:03:00.370 --> 01:03:23.010
[INAUDIBLE]

01:03:23.010 --> 01:03:26.170
So the question is, when
you do this alignment,

01:03:26.170 --> 01:03:29.530
how does it know what the
language-- is the model language

01:03:29.530 --> 01:03:31.350
model pre-trained?

01:03:31.350 --> 01:03:34.930
And it definitely has to be
pre-trained because otherwise it

01:03:34.930 --> 01:03:36.710
doesn't make sense to align it.

01:03:36.710 --> 01:03:38.510
So the language model is frozen.

01:03:38.510 --> 01:03:40.370
And you're just
training the adapter

01:03:40.370 --> 01:03:47.330
to connect the given vision
encoder with a given language

01:03:47.330 --> 01:03:48.210
model.

01:03:48.210 --> 01:03:50.350
And the way you
train it is, well,

01:03:50.350 --> 01:03:54.810
you pick a token budget,
67 billion tokens

01:03:54.810 --> 01:03:56.150
and you just train.

01:03:56.150 --> 01:04:00.090
There's not an adaptive
threshold here.

01:04:00.090 --> 01:04:00.610
Yeah.

01:04:00.610 --> 01:04:32.340
[INAUDIBLE]

01:04:32.340 --> 01:04:36.220
Yeah, so the question
is, is the number

01:04:36.220 --> 01:04:39.220
of parameters in the
vision coder much smaller?

01:04:39.220 --> 01:04:41.820
And the answer is
yes, in general.

01:04:41.820 --> 01:04:43.700
I'm trying to find--

01:04:43.700 --> 01:04:45.520
where is the other place I--

01:04:48.820 --> 01:04:49.640
not here.

01:04:49.640 --> 01:04:54.580
OK, the reason is that
the vision encoder

01:04:54.580 --> 01:04:57.960
is in some sense doing
a very local operation.

01:04:57.960 --> 01:04:58.960
It's looking at a patch.

01:04:58.960 --> 01:05:00.460
A patch is very small.

01:05:00.460 --> 01:05:03.500
And it's just trying to
understand the patches.

01:05:03.500 --> 01:05:05.480
There's not much knowledge.

01:05:05.480 --> 01:05:07.460
So the patch, we're
not reasoning.

01:05:07.460 --> 01:05:10.200
So most of the
capabilities of the model

01:05:10.200 --> 01:05:14.560
are still in the language model.

01:05:14.560 --> 01:05:17.680
I'm trying to find the--

01:05:17.680 --> 01:05:19.240
OK, where?

01:05:19.240 --> 01:05:21.440
Maybe it was up here.

01:05:21.440 --> 01:05:23.680
OK, I can't-- oh, yeah.

01:05:23.680 --> 01:05:28.160
So if you look at the
LLaVA model, so the full--

01:05:28.160 --> 01:05:30.720
so 72 billion parameter
language model.

01:05:35.640 --> 01:05:36.160
Sorry.

01:05:36.160 --> 01:05:46.220
What-- OK, I don't know why
this number is so-- oh, sorry.

01:05:46.220 --> 01:05:47.700
This is 72 million, OK.

01:05:47.700 --> 01:05:49.980
So this is much smaller.

01:05:49.980 --> 01:05:51.380
The projector is much smaller.

01:05:51.380 --> 01:05:58.680
And the VIT is generally less
than a billion, parameters.

01:05:58.680 --> 01:06:05.000
Yeah, I guess mostly because
it's fairly a local operation.

01:06:05.000 --> 01:06:07.380
I guess that's the
easy way to say it.

01:06:07.380 --> 01:06:09.400
OK, I'm going to think
some other questions.

01:06:09.400 --> 01:06:11.360
I'm going to try to move on now.

01:06:13.870 --> 01:06:18.860
So I Qwen-3 is the last
vision language model.

01:06:18.860 --> 01:06:24.340
I'll talk about this SOTA,
Save Our Performance.

01:06:24.340 --> 01:06:28.660
There's a lot of data
work that happens.

01:06:28.660 --> 01:06:31.060
Not too many details
about the data

01:06:31.060 --> 01:06:35.580
and the data mix in
these later Qwen models.

01:06:35.580 --> 01:06:41.580
So you'll have to look at
the LLaVA paper or the--

01:06:41.580 --> 01:06:43.620
AI2 has a [INAUDIBLE]
paper, which

01:06:43.620 --> 01:06:45.980
I didn't get a chance
to talk about that

01:06:45.980 --> 01:06:49.420
has more details here.

01:06:49.420 --> 01:06:56.660
And from Qwen to Qwen-2, Qwen-3,
many of these changes were

01:06:56.660 --> 01:06:59.285
mostly--

01:06:59.285 --> 01:07:01.910
there's some changes there.

01:07:01.910 --> 01:07:06.110
But the overall framework
remains the same.

01:07:06.110 --> 01:07:09.690
And mostly, it's scaling
up, curating more data sets,

01:07:09.690 --> 01:07:13.630
noticing that your context
isn't-- you have to handle long

01:07:13.630 --> 01:07:18.990
context and maybe sharpening
your image processing and so on.

01:07:18.990 --> 01:07:26.550
OK, so I'm going to now jump
to something quite different.

01:07:26.550 --> 01:07:32.110
So there's this paper called
Chameleon from Meta in 2024.

01:07:32.110 --> 01:07:37.370
So, so far, VLMs encode
images into these vectors

01:07:37.370 --> 01:07:40.550
and then they inject them
into a language model.

01:07:40.550 --> 01:07:42.430
And because it's
a language model,

01:07:42.430 --> 01:07:44.890
you can only generate text.

01:07:44.890 --> 01:07:49.550
You can't generate images.

01:07:49.550 --> 01:07:53.850
So there's many ways you
can go about fixing this.

01:07:53.850 --> 01:07:55.530
You can just take a VLM.

01:07:55.530 --> 01:07:57.570
You can also attach
a diffusion head.

01:07:57.570 --> 01:08:00.170
And now you can
generate images or--

01:08:00.170 --> 01:08:04.170
but I want to talk
about this idea here,

01:08:04.170 --> 01:08:07.570
which is Chameleon says,
what if we mapped everything

01:08:07.570 --> 01:08:10.450
into discrete tokens?

01:08:10.450 --> 01:08:14.210
And in some ways
aesthetically this is--

01:08:14.210 --> 01:08:16.430
well, maybe this reflects
that I'm a language person.

01:08:16.430 --> 01:08:22.170
This is appealing because now
you can analyze and generate

01:08:22.170 --> 01:08:23.569
images in the same way.

01:08:23.569 --> 01:08:25.370
Everything is a discrete token.

01:08:25.370 --> 01:08:30.410
If you want to do what--

01:08:30.410 --> 01:08:33.550
let's say you want
to generate images.

01:08:33.550 --> 01:08:37.229
You can say, here's
a text prompt.

01:08:37.229 --> 01:08:40.649
And then you can just--
these are more just

01:08:40.649 --> 01:08:42.330
tokens that you can generate.

01:08:42.330 --> 01:08:49.330
Or you can flip the language
and the text around as well.

01:08:49.330 --> 01:08:52.770
They have these examples
from the Chameleon paper

01:08:52.770 --> 01:08:55.319
you can prompt in say I'm bored.

01:08:55.319 --> 01:08:56.479
Can you show me some birds?

01:08:56.479 --> 01:08:58.220
And then it would
be text, and then it

01:08:58.220 --> 01:09:01.439
would be images, and then some
more text and some images.

01:09:01.439 --> 01:09:03.979
So it's interleaved.

01:09:03.979 --> 01:09:06.899
So in some sense, the
vision of an omni model

01:09:06.899 --> 01:09:10.800
is that text and images
truly live in the same space.

01:09:10.800 --> 01:09:14.020
And this is accomplished
by making everything look

01:09:14.020 --> 01:09:15.000
like text.

01:09:15.000 --> 01:09:16.620
That's one way to do it.

01:09:16.620 --> 01:09:20.120
OK, so I'll talk a little
bit about how this is done.

01:09:20.120 --> 01:09:28.380
So the thing you need to
do now is to map images

01:09:28.380 --> 01:09:30.740
into discrete tokens.

01:09:30.740 --> 01:09:38.460
So there's this idea
rather old from Oord 2017,

01:09:38.460 --> 01:09:41.740
which is called vector quantized
variational autoencoder.

01:09:41.740 --> 01:09:47.180
And the basic idea
here is to map an image

01:09:47.180 --> 01:09:51.060
into a discrete code.

01:09:51.060 --> 01:09:54.080
And what you learn is a
mapping that maps this

01:09:54.080 --> 01:09:57.080
into a continuous vector.

01:09:57.080 --> 01:10:00.840
And this gets rounded
to the nearest code.

01:10:00.840 --> 01:10:06.060
So you have a codebook which
has, let's say, 8,000 codes.

01:10:06.060 --> 01:10:11.040
So these are prototypical
vectors that correspond to what

01:10:11.040 --> 01:10:12.320
patches.

01:10:12.320 --> 01:10:16.600
And then so you pick one of
these that's the closest.

01:10:16.600 --> 01:10:19.120
And that would be
your representation.

01:10:19.120 --> 01:10:20.163
So that's your encoder.

01:10:20.163 --> 01:10:21.580
It goes through a
continuous phase

01:10:21.580 --> 01:10:25.080
and then you round it
to the nearest code.

01:10:25.080 --> 01:10:27.640
And now the decoder
is you take that code

01:10:27.640 --> 01:10:29.800
and you try to
reconstruct the image.

01:10:29.800 --> 01:10:36.480
And then you train these
VQ-VAEs by basically minimizing

01:10:36.480 --> 01:10:38.680
the reconstruction loss.

01:10:38.680 --> 01:10:40.240
There are some
other terms that you

01:10:40.240 --> 01:10:43.160
add because this is not
differentiable, which

01:10:43.160 --> 01:10:46.640
I won't have time to get into.

01:10:46.640 --> 01:10:52.500
OK, but, at the end of the day,
you have 512 by 512 images that

01:10:52.500 --> 01:10:59.980
are converted into 1,024 tokens,
and each token comes from

01:10:59.980 --> 01:11:03.140
a vocabulary of 8,000.

01:11:03.140 --> 01:11:06.440
OK, so now you have
basically text.

01:11:06.440 --> 01:11:12.020
You have your images, which
are basically these codes.

01:11:12.020 --> 01:11:16.820
They train a new
tokenizer because now you

01:11:16.820 --> 01:11:19.460
have different-- your data
looks different than if it were

01:11:19.460 --> 01:11:24.060
just normal natural language.

01:11:24.060 --> 01:11:29.520
The training is now actually
very straightforward.

01:11:29.520 --> 01:11:31.880
This is just normal
language model training.

01:11:31.880 --> 01:11:32.920
There's no adapter.

01:11:32.920 --> 01:11:36.860
There's no turn
the vision encoder.

01:11:36.860 --> 01:11:39.940
There's just a
language model now.

01:11:39.940 --> 01:11:44.020
So this is the part
that's appealing.

01:11:44.020 --> 01:11:46.560
So there's still two stages.

01:11:46.560 --> 01:11:48.420
But even for language
model training,

01:11:48.420 --> 01:11:51.030
there's usually
two or more stages,

01:11:51.030 --> 01:11:53.690
where the first stage
is the bulk of training.

01:11:53.690 --> 01:11:55.710
It's largely unsupervised.

01:11:55.710 --> 01:11:59.950
They have both text
and text and image.

01:11:59.950 --> 01:12:04.950
And then stage 2, they
mix in high quality data.

01:12:04.950 --> 01:12:09.430
OK, so this is really
great and elegant.

01:12:09.430 --> 01:12:11.750
There's a few
problems with this.

01:12:11.750 --> 01:12:16.510
One is that they found that
the training was not stable.

01:12:16.510 --> 01:12:20.110
And the reason is
that text and images,

01:12:20.110 --> 01:12:22.550
despite occupying
the same space,

01:12:22.550 --> 01:12:25.230
just behave very differently.

01:12:25.230 --> 01:12:30.530
So just calling
things discrete tokens

01:12:30.530 --> 01:12:33.630
isn't hiding the fact that
there's an image living there.

01:12:33.630 --> 01:12:36.590
And in particular,
text tokens, if you

01:12:36.590 --> 01:12:38.410
think about predicting
the next word,

01:12:38.410 --> 01:12:40.890
this has relatively
lower entropy.

01:12:40.890 --> 01:12:45.510
Most words are predictable,
whereas image tokens

01:12:45.510 --> 01:12:46.810
have very high entropy.

01:12:46.810 --> 01:12:50.970
I don't know what shade of blue
this exact token is going to be.

01:12:50.970 --> 01:12:55.890
And so as a result, they found
that training with this mixture

01:12:55.890 --> 01:12:59.970
led to a lot of the
norms of the parameters

01:12:59.970 --> 01:13:05.890
to grow and to generate
loss instability.

01:13:05.890 --> 01:13:08.290
They were able to
mitigate or fix this

01:13:08.290 --> 01:13:12.810
to some extent via QK norm and
z-loss regularization, which

01:13:12.810 --> 01:13:16.870
controls the norm growth.

01:13:16.870 --> 01:13:20.310
So I'm not even going
to show the results.

01:13:20.310 --> 01:13:24.210
But I wanted to highlight
this work because there's

01:13:24.210 --> 01:13:28.210
a certain elegance here because
it's just one model that treats

01:13:28.210 --> 01:13:31.330
really all modalities the same.

01:13:31.330 --> 01:13:35.010
But the downside is
that it turned out

01:13:35.010 --> 01:13:38.210
this model was not
really as performant,

01:13:38.210 --> 01:13:42.890
and the discretization does
definitely lose its information.

01:13:42.890 --> 01:13:46.590
So think about OCR.

01:13:46.590 --> 01:13:51.090
Again, if you discretize
very small print,

01:13:51.090 --> 01:13:54.030
you're not going to be
able to read it anymore.

01:13:54.030 --> 01:13:57.570
And finally, training with
multi modalities is also tricky.

01:13:57.570 --> 01:13:59.870
We saw this a little
bit with the Qwen models

01:13:59.870 --> 01:14:02.130
and we had to play
around with the weights.

01:14:02.130 --> 01:14:05.350
But this is more
exacerbated here.

01:14:05.350 --> 01:14:10.430
So VQ-VAEs were
popular for a while.

01:14:10.430 --> 01:14:15.690
And in fact, many people were
using this for image generation.

01:14:15.690 --> 01:14:19.525
The main reason to do that is,
well, you have a transformer.

01:14:19.525 --> 01:14:21.150
How do you generate
from a transformer?

01:14:21.150 --> 01:14:22.990
Well, you have to
generate discrete things

01:14:22.990 --> 01:14:28.510
and you basically put all your
data into this discrete form.

01:14:28.510 --> 01:14:32.230
But then diffusion
models came out

01:14:32.230 --> 01:14:35.410
and became popular and
viable for generation.

01:14:35.410 --> 01:14:43.070
So this flavor of method is
less popular than it used to be.

01:14:43.070 --> 01:14:48.560
OK, so maybe just reflect
and summarize here.

01:14:48.560 --> 01:14:50.920
Frontier models these
days are expected

01:14:50.920 --> 01:14:55.640
to be multimodal or even more
strongly natively multimodal

01:14:55.640 --> 01:14:58.000
or omni models.

01:14:58.000 --> 01:15:01.560
Unfortunately, I think
when Gemini comes out

01:15:01.560 --> 01:15:04.440
or GPT comes out,
they tout or touted

01:15:04.440 --> 01:15:07.900
as being natively multimodal
and handle all these modalities.

01:15:07.900 --> 01:15:09.425
And in fact, they do.

01:15:09.425 --> 01:15:10.800
But of course,
there's no details

01:15:10.800 --> 01:15:14.240
about how these are built.

01:15:14.240 --> 01:15:18.840
I think that it's
probably combination

01:15:18.840 --> 01:15:22.840
of having a continuous
encoder because you don't want

01:15:22.840 --> 01:15:26.400
to lose information and then
diffusion for the generation

01:15:26.400 --> 01:15:31.000
because-- but that's
my speculation.

01:15:31.000 --> 01:15:32.897
The fundamental
challenge, I think,

01:15:32.897 --> 01:15:34.480
when you're dealing
with multimodality

01:15:34.480 --> 01:15:39.360
is how do you handle
non-text modalities?

01:15:39.360 --> 01:15:40.920
It's also interesting
that there's

01:15:40.920 --> 01:15:44.260
a asymmetry between
understanding the modality

01:15:44.260 --> 01:15:45.540
and generation.

01:15:45.540 --> 01:15:49.700
And there's no one
universal encoder let's say.

01:15:49.700 --> 01:15:53.060
For example, when
we looked at CLIP,

01:15:53.060 --> 01:15:55.300
you only cared
for classification

01:15:55.300 --> 01:15:57.080
to capture high level semantics.

01:15:57.080 --> 01:15:59.920
So these vectors
could be fairly small

01:15:59.920 --> 01:16:02.380
and capture the high
level semantics.

01:16:02.380 --> 01:16:05.980
Whereas if you wanted to do
OCR or whether you wanted

01:16:05.980 --> 01:16:09.780
to generate an image, then you
need really fine-grained detail.

01:16:09.780 --> 01:16:12.740
You need to-- that's why
diffusion is so good because it

01:16:12.740 --> 01:16:20.673
can really micro optimize the
low-high frequency information.

01:16:20.673 --> 01:16:22.340
I think, in general,
when you're dealing

01:16:22.340 --> 01:16:26.020
with multiple modalities,
you have to think carefully

01:16:26.020 --> 01:16:30.820
about weighing them properly.

01:16:30.820 --> 01:16:34.220
For example, video certainly
has lower information density

01:16:34.220 --> 01:16:35.740
than text.

01:16:35.740 --> 01:16:41.590
So you don't want a video
to overwhelm your text.

01:16:41.590 --> 01:16:47.710
And like I said before, at
least the current best thing

01:16:47.710 --> 01:16:52.190
is having continuous encoders.

01:16:52.190 --> 01:16:55.270
It seems even CLIP, even
though it's five years old,

01:16:55.270 --> 01:16:59.510
or similar ideas are
still the go-to way

01:16:59.510 --> 01:17:03.630
to capture semantics of images.

01:17:03.630 --> 01:17:05.590
Transformers are still there.

01:17:05.590 --> 01:17:08.350
And then diffusion models,
which I didn't talk about,

01:17:08.350 --> 01:17:12.910
are great for generation.

01:17:12.910 --> 01:17:16.430
OK, so I will stop there.

01:17:16.430 --> 01:17:22.870
That's it for the
lecture on multimodality.

01:17:22.870 --> 01:17:25.730
I guess we don't have any
homework on doing this.

01:17:25.730 --> 01:17:29.430
But if you're curious,
I think I encourage

01:17:29.430 --> 01:17:34.060
you to play around with
training some of these models.

